## 2. Running Two DC Motors with an L298N Driver Module

### 2.1 What the L298N Does

The **L298N** is a dual H‑bridge driver that lets you control **direction** and **speed** of two DC motors (or one stepper) with just a few Arduino pins. Inside each H‑bridge are four transistors arranged so the motor can be driven forward or backward, and an **enable** pin that receives PWM for speed control.

> **Analogy:** Think of the L298N as a small traffic controller that can open and close four lanes (the transistors) to let current flow in either direction, while a separate “gate” (enable) throttles how much traffic passes.

### 2.2 Parts List

| Part | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| L298N driver board (with heat sink) | Dual H‑bridge |
| Two small DC motors (≈ 6 V) | Loads |
| 12 V wall adapter or 9 V battery pack | Motor supply (V S) |
| Jumper wires & breadboard | Connections |
| Optional: 10 µF capacitor across V S and GND | Reduces voltage ripple |

### 2.3 Wiring Overview

| Arduino Pin | L298N Connection | Function |
|-------------|------------------|----------|
| D3 (PWM)    | ENA (Enable A)   | Speed of Motor A |
| D4          | IN1              | Direction A (forward) |
| D5          | IN2              | Direction A (reverse) |
| D6 (PWM)    | ENB (Enable B)   | Speed of Motor B |
| D7          | IN3              | Direction B (forward) |
| D8          | IN4              | Direction B (reverse) |
| GND         | GND              | Common ground |
| VIN (or external) | V S            | Motor power (9‑12 V) |

> **Note:** The L298N also provides a **5 V regulator** that can power the Arduino, but for learning clarity we’ll keep the Arduino powered via USB or its own supply.

### 2.4 Sample Code: Two‑Motor Differential Drive

```cpp
// Pin assignments
const int ENA = 3;   // PWM for left motor
const int IN1 = 4;
const int IN2 = 5;

const int ENB = 6;   // PWM for right motor
const int IN3 = 7;
const int IN4 = 8;

void setup() {
  pinMode(ENA, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(ENB, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN1, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN2, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN3, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(IN4, OUTPUT);
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  // Move forward at 75% speed
  digitalWrite(IN1, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(IN2, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN3, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(IN4, LOW);
  analogWrite(ENA, 192);   // 192/255 ≈ 75%
  analogWrite(ENB, 192);
  delay(2000);

  // Turn left (left motor slower)
  analogWrite(ENA, 80);    // Slow left wheel
  analogWrite(ENB, 192);   // Fast right wheel
  delay(1500);

  // Reverse
  digitalWrite(IN1, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN2, HIGH);
  digitalWrite(IN3, LOW);
  digitalWrite(IN4, HIGH);
  analogWrite(ENA, 180);
  analogWrite(ENB, 180);
  delay(2000);
}
```

### 2.5 Why Use a Driver Instead of a Single Transistor?

- **Two motors**: One driver handles both, saving pins and wiring.  
- **Higher current**: The L298N can safely switch up to 2 A per channel—more than a single transistor can usually handle.  
- **Built‑in flyback protection**: The module already includes diodes, so you don’t need to add external ones.

