## 4. Adding a Potentiometer for Motor Speed Control

### 4.1 The Idea

A **potentiometer** (variable resistor) can act as a simple, tactile speed knob. By reading its position with `analogRead` and feeding the result into `analogWrite`, you create a **real‑time speed controller**.

### 4.2 Wiring the Potentiometer

| Pin | Connection |
|-----|------------|
| Middle (wiper) | Arduino **A0** (or any analog input) |
| One outer pin   | **5 V** |
| Other outer pin | **GND** |

### 4.3 Integrating with the Single‑Motor Transistor Circuit

```cpp
const int motorPin = 9;      // PWM to transistor
const int dirPin   = 8;      // Direction control
const int potPin   = A0;     // Speed knob

void setup() {
  pinMode(motorPin, OUTPUT);
  pinMode(dirPin,   OUTPUT);
  Serial.begin(9600);
}

void loop() {
  int potVal = analogRead(potPin);                     // 0‑1023
  int speed  = map(potVal, 0, 1023, 0, 255);           // PWM range
  analogWrite(motorPin, speed);
  Serial.print("Pot: "); Serial.print(potVal);
  Serial.print("  PWM: "); Serial.println(speed);
  delay(10);
}
```

*Now turning the knob continuously changes the motor’s speed. Combine this with the direction pin to reverse the motor on a button press (e.g., using a momentary push‑button on a digital pin).*

### 4.4 Using the Potentiometer with the L298N

Replace the `analogWrite` lines in the dual‑motor sketch with the mapped potentiometer value. Because the L298N has separate enable pins for each motor, you can control **both wheels** with the same speed knob—perfect for a simple “drive‑forward” robot.

```cpp
int speed = map(analogRead(potPin), 0, 1023, 0, 255);
analogWrite(ENA, speed);
analogWrite(ENB, speed);
```

