## OLED Displays: Small Yet Powerful  

### OLED Basics  

OLED (Organic Light‑Emitting Diode) displays consist of tiny organic pixels that emit light when current passes through them. Unlike LCDs, they do **not** need a backlight, giving them deep blacks and excellent contrast. Common sizes for Arduino projects are **0.96‑inch 128×64** and **1.3‑inch 128×64**.

### Wiring Options  

OLED modules are usually sold with either **I²C** or **SPI** interfaces. The I²C version uses the same two pins as the LCD, while SPI adds two more pins for faster data transfer.

| OLED Pin | Arduino Pin (I²C) | Arduino Pin (SPI) |
|----------|-------------------|-------------------|
| VCC      | 3.3 V (or 5 V)    | 3.3 V (or 5 V)    |
| GND      | GND               | GND               |
| SDA      | A4                | MOSI (D11)        |
| SCL      | A5                | SCK  (D13)        |
| RES      | –                 | Reset (any digital) |
| DC       | –                 | Data/Command (any digital) |
| CS*      | –                 | Chip Select (any digital) |

> *CS* (Chip Select) is required only for SPI; for I²C you can leave it unconnected.

### Installing the Libraries  

The **Adafruit_SSD1306** library works together with **Adafruit_GFX** (a graphics core). Install both via the Library Manager.

### Drawing Shapes, Text, and Icons  

The GFX library gives you drawing primitives similar to those in Processing or p5.js. Below is a minimal sketch that draws a temperature gauge:

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <Adafruit_GFX.h>
#include <Adafruit_SSD1306.h>

#define SCREEN_WIDTH 128   // OLED width, in pixels
#define SCREEN_HEIGHT 64   // OLED height, in pixels
#define OLED_RESET    -1   // Use -1 if reset pin not connected

Adafruit_SSD1306 display(SCREEN_WIDTH, SCREEN_HEIGHT, &Wire, OLED_RESET);

void setup() {
  display.begin(SSD1306_SWITCHCAPVCC, 0x3C); // 0x3C is common address
  display.clearDisplay();
  display.setTextSize(1);
  display.setTextColor(SSD1306_WHITE);
  display.setCursor(0,0);
  display.println("Temp Gauge");
  display.display();          // Push buffer to screen
}

void loop() {
  float tempC = readTemp();   // Assume a function that returns °C
  drawGauge(tempC);
  delay(1000);
}

void drawGauge(float t) {
  display.clearDisplay();
  display.setCursor(0,0);
  display.print("Temp: ");
  display.print(t,1);
  display.print((char)247);   // Degree symbol
  display.println("C");

  // Draw a simple bar proportional to temperature (0‑50 °C)
  int barWidth = map(t, 0, 50, 0, 100);
  display.fillRect(0, 30, barWidth, 10, SSD1306_WHITE);
  display.display();
}
```

**What the code does**

* `display.clearDisplay()` clears the internal buffer (not the physical screen).  
* `display.display()` transfers the buffer to the OLED – you can update the screen many times per second without flicker.  
* `fillRect()` draws a filled rectangle, useful for progress bars or simple gauges.

### Adding Custom Icons  

You can convert a 16×16 bitmap (e.g., a snowflake) into a PROGMEM array using the **Arduino Image Converter** online. Then use `display.drawBitmap(x, y, bitmap, w, h, color)` to render it.

